"""Original knowledge-governance teaching; no source exercises adapted."""
def course(c,m):
    return c("SWP-047","Indigenous Knowledge and Community Stewardship","indigenous-knowledge-and-community-stewardship","Indigenous Knowledge\nand Community\nStewardship",
        "Develop a community-agreed stewardship learning plan with bounded knowledge permissions, meaningful influence and accountable sharing and review arrangements.",[
m("Knowledge holders identity and authority","Identify whose knowledge and authority matter without assuming a single community voice or unrestricted access.",[
"Indigenous knowledge and local knowledge are related terms with distinct histories and relationships to identity, land, institutions and authority. Do not assume that every local practice is Indigenous or assign Indigenous identity to a Nigerian community because a project uses that term. Ask how people describe their knowledge and which protocols govern an intended engagement. Dorji and colleagues review diverse reported practices in selected English-language literature; the review cannot identify this community's knowledge holders or determine whether a practice should be transferred. Begin with respectful enquiry and the relevant local relationships, not a universal cultural checklist. [R5, S1]",
"Communities contain different experiences and forms of authority. A recognised leader may help identify a legitimate process but should not automatically be treated as the sole voice on every subject or as able to consent for every individual. Ask who should contribute, whose perspective is missing and how people can decline without penalty. The participation review highlights power and exclusion across a broad literature, while the fairness of this engagement needs direct checking. Schedule and language arrangements should allow meaningful influence rather than merely increase an attendance count after the decision has already been fixed. [R2, S1]",
"Knowledge may be public, shared for a specific purpose or restricted by the people responsible for it. Permission to discuss a practice in a meeting does not necessarily include recording it, publishing a location, distributing a transcript or entering it into an artificial intelligence service. GIDA's governance resource and CARE principles emphasise collective benefit, authority, responsibility and ethics. They inform questions for agreement; they do not replace local protocols, individual rights or applicable law. Clarify the proposed use before requesting information and accept that some information should not be requested or recorded at all. [S8, S1]",
"Acknowledge knowledge with neither automatic dismissal nor automatic endorsement. A reported environmental practice may contain valuable observations while still requiring context-specific ecological, health or technical assessment before an activity is proposed. The climate-services review examines how knowledge is operationalised across roles and projects, not whether any particular practice is safe everywhere. Record what a contributor has authorised you to learn, what remains outside scope and the next decision that the knowledge could inform. Explain the limits of your own role and avoid promising a technical result or unrestricted publication in exchange for participation. [R7, R5, S1]"],
"Fictional case: a stewardship team is invited to discuss seasonal observations. A local contact permits a discussion but says some locations must not be recorded. The team asks about an agreed participation process, offers ways to decline and does not treat the invitation as permission for public mapping or automated transcription.",
"Prepare an engagement note covering self-description, relevant knowledge holders, missing voices, the decision open to influence and permitted uses. Identify one type of information the team should avoid collecting until permission and necessity are clear.",
"The note should recognise plural authority and bounded permission. It must not infer Indigenous identity, universal validity or unrestricted sharing from a local practice, a meeting invitation or one person's agreement.",["R5","S1","R2","S8","R7"],[
("Indigenous and local knowledge are not interchangeable labels.","A team labels all local observations Indigenous without asking contributors. What should change?","Clarify people's own descriptions and relevant histories and protocols.",["Keep the label because any local activity proves Indigenous identity.","Ask an external database to assign an identity automatically.","Assume the label grants permission to publish."],"Identity and knowledge relationships should not be imposed by a project label."),
("Authority can be plural and topic-specific.","One contact approves a meeting. What is still necessary before claiming community agreement on every use?","Check the appropriate decision process, other voices and the scope of authority.",["Treat the approval as consent from every individual.","Assume authority is identical for every topic.","Exclude anyone who questions the proposal."],"An invitation does not establish unlimited representative authority."),
("Discussion permission does not authorise every subsequent use.","Contributors allow a private discussion but not publication of locations. Which action respects the boundary?","Keep locations out of public outputs and clarify any further proposed use.",["Publish the map because discussion occurred.","Upload all notes to a public AI tool without checking.","Assume a credit line overrides the restriction."],"Permission is bounded by the agreed purpose and use."),
("Participation should allow meaningful influence.","A team seeks local input only after choosing the only acceptable option. What needs examination?","Whether contributors have an honest opportunity to influence a decision still open.",["Whether attendance alone proves influence.","Whether a longer sign-in sheet resolves the issue.","Whether critical contributors can be removed."],"Influence depends on actual decision space and inclusion."),
("Reported practices need contextual assessment.","A review describes a practice used elsewhere. What does that establish for this site's proposed activity?","A question for contextual investigation rather than proof of local safety or effectiveness.",["Universal safety in every setting.","Automatic authority to prescribe the practice.","Permission to disclose all associated knowledge."],"A reported practice is neither a universal intervention nor a permission grant."),
]),
m("Co-designing stewardship and learning","Plan a bounded stewardship learning activity around an agreed decision and context-specific evidence.",[
"Begin co-design by identifying a decision that contributors consider useful and that the responsible roles can actually influence. A learning activity might explore how to review access to an existing community resource, rather than extract a catalogue of everything people know. Agree the purpose, boundaries, expected benefit and who will respond to findings before requesting contributions. The climate-services review connects knowledge use to decisions and relationships among service roles; it does not prove that collecting more observations automatically improves an outcome. Explain what will remain undecided and how contributors can challenge the proposed purpose. [R7, S1]",
"Create participation arrangements that fit the people involved. Language, timing, disability access, caring responsibilities and differences between generations can affect who is able to contribute. Ask what adjustments are appropriate instead of assuming one meeting format will work for everyone. Use separate or alternative channels where a collective discussion would suppress a view or expose sensitive information. The participation literature supports examining influence and power, but no standard checklist can certify this meeting as inclusive. Record missing perspectives and agreed attempts to address them without identifying people unnecessarily or interpreting non-attendance as consent. [R2, S1]",
"Select a small learning activity with the relevant community and responsible technical roles. Distinguish observing or discussing an existing arrangement from authorising ecological alteration, health treatment or construction. The knowledge review describes diverse practices under particular conditions; it should not be used to prescribe a technique from another place. Identify what needs specialist assessment, which permissions apply and what could cause harm or transfer a burden. If an activity cannot proceed within those conditions, revise its scope or keep it at the discussion stage instead of presenting an unapproved experiment as community stewardship. [R5, R7, S1]",
"Agree what success would mean for the bounded activity and how contributors will review it. A useful outcome may be a more accessible decision process or a clearer response to a priority, while meeting attendance is only an output. Choose proportionate evidence, note assumptions and provide feedback in an accessible form that contributors have approved. Do not require disclosure of restricted knowledge to demonstrate participation. GIDA's principles direct attention to community benefit and responsibility; the local agreement must still specify who can act, how a concern is raised and whether the activity remains worthwhile to those contributing. [S8, R2, S1]"],
"Fictional case: contributors prefer to review access to an existing shared resource rather than disclose restricted seasonal sites. The team agrees a discussion-only activity, checks who is missing and asks the appropriate technical role about any later physical change. Feedback will use an approved summary without locations.",
"Design a one-page learning plan with purpose, decision space, participation adjustments, knowledge outside scope, technical dependency, outcome question and feedback owner. Include a condition under which the activity should be revised.",
"A defensible plan begins with an agreed useful decision, respects non-disclosure and keeps technical approval separate. Its evidence should concern the intended benefit rather than treat attendance or information volume as success.",["R7","S1","R2","R5","S8"],[
("Knowledge collection should serve an agreed useful decision.","A project requests every local observation before explaining any intended use. What should happen first?","Agree the purpose, decision space, boundaries and expected benefit.",["Collect everything because storage is inexpensive.","Promise that information volume guarantees adaptation.","Assume all contributors want the same output."],"Purpose and authority should precede information collection."),
("Participation formats affect whose views are heard.","An evening meeting excludes people with care responsibilities. What is a useful response?","Discuss feasible alternative arrangements and record the remaining gap.",["Interpret absence as agreement.","Assume the same format suits every contributor.","Publish the names of non-attendees as uncooperative."],"Access and power require practical attention without punitive disclosure."),
("A discussion does not authorise ecological alteration.","A learning group proposes changing a watercourse after discussing observations. What is needed?","The relevant permissions and qualified technical assessment before any physical change.",["A general journal citation as construction authority.","A majority vote replacing all technical review.","An immediate unsupervised experiment."],"Learning contributions do not replace required authority or expertise."),
("Restricted knowledge need not be disclosed to prove participation.","A contributor declines to reveal a sensitive site but offers a general concern. What is appropriate?","Respect the boundary and consider the concern within the agreed purpose.",["Require the site location as evidence of commitment.","Guess and publish the location.","Treat refusal as permission to record covertly."],"Participation must respect the scope and limits of sharing."),
("Outcomes concern the intended change.","A stewardship activity aims to improve access to a decision process. Which question addresses that outcome?","Can intended contributors understand and influence the relevant decision?",["How many sheets were printed?","How many observation files were stored?","How many decorative posters were displayed?"],"Process access and influence are distinct from production counts."),
]),
m("Knowledge governance sharing and accountability","Document permitted uses and accountable review without treating attribution as consent.",[
"A knowledge-use agreement should describe the specific purpose, responsible roles, intended users, access restrictions, retention arrangements and approved outputs. Use language people can understand and the relevant local decision process. Some matters may require collective governance as well as individual agreement; one does not automatically replace the other. GIDA's CARE framework helps frame these questions, while local authority and legal obligations still need checking. Do not present an introductory template as a universal contract. Where permission is unclear, narrow the proposed collection or use while the appropriate process is clarified. [S8, S1]",
"Attribution acknowledges a contribution but does not create permission to use it. Ask whether and how contributors want to be identified, including whether an aggregate acknowledgement could still expose a small group. A source being publicly visible also does not necessarily authorise every reuse, adaptation or redistribution. In this course, the GIDA resource is linked and discussed through original commentary; its slides and exercises are not reproduced or adapted. Apply the same discipline to local contributions: record the agreed use and restrictions, and seek further agreement when a proposed output changes scope. [S8, S1]",
"Treat digital sharing as a separate decision with practical consequences. A map, photograph or transcript can expose locations, identities or relationships beyond the original audience. Do not enter restricted material into an unapproved AI or cloud service, or assume removing a name removes identification risk. Check necessity, approved access and the ability to control later distribution. The knowledge and participation reviews highlight relationships and influence, not a technological permission shortcut. A useful summary may answer the agreed decision question without retaining or disclosing the underlying sensitive details at all. [R7, R2, S1]",
"Provide a route for contributors to review proposed outputs, correct inaccuracies and raise concerns about later use. Explain honestly what can be changed, withdrawn or deleted within the relevant agreement and obligations, including limits once approved information has been distributed. Record the responsible role, response, version and communication back to contributors. Accountability includes reporting what happened after people contributed, not simply collecting a signature at the beginning. Review whether the intended benefit materialised and whose perspective is still missing; a respectful learning process can require changing or stopping a proposed use when its conditions are no longer satisfied. [S8, R5, S1]"],
"Fictional case: a team has permission for an internal learning summary, with contributor review before circulation. A sponsor later requests an online map and named quotations. The team treats these as new proposed uses, checks authority and restrictions, and retains the original limits while that decision is unresolved.",
"Write a knowledge-use record covering purpose, authority, access, retention, approved output, attribution preference, review route and a proposed scope change. Explain one limit to withdrawal that must be communicated honestly.",
"The record should make permissions specific and revisable through an accountable process. Acknowledgement, technical convenience or a sponsor's request cannot substitute for the appropriate agreement and lawful obligations.",["S8","S1","R7","R2","R5"],[
("Collective governance and individual rights need attention.","A project has one person's consent for material governed by a collective protocol. What should be checked?","The relevant collective authority as well as the individual's rights and agreement.",["That individual consent automatically cancels every collective protocol.","That collective approval cancels every individual's rights.","That no authority question remains because collection is convenient."],"Different rights and governance responsibilities may apply together."),
("Attribution is distinct from permission.","A team offers to credit a contributor for an unapproved public output. What remains necessary?","The appropriate permission for that specific use.",["Only a larger credit line.","An assumption that acknowledgement authorises any use.","A decision to publish before asking."],"Recognition does not grant a use right."),
("Removing a name may not prevent identification.","A small community's sensitive site is mapped without personal names. What risk remains?","Location and context may still identify people or restricted knowledge.",["All identification risk is automatically removed.","Maps cannot reveal relationships.","Publication restrictions no longer apply."],"Indirect identifiers and context can expose sensitive information."),
("A changed output requires renewed scope review.","Permission covers an internal summary but a sponsor requests a public transcript. What is appropriate?","Treat the transcript as a new proposed use and check the relevant agreement.",["Assume funding creates unrestricted publication authority.","Publish because a summary was previously allowed.","Remove all recorded permission limits."],"A wider audience and different output can exceed the original scope."),
("Accountability continues after initial agreement.","An approved output contains an error raised by contributors. What should the process include?","Review, correction where appropriate, a response and a retained amendment history.",["Ignoring concerns because an initial signature exists.","Secret replacement without any response.","A guarantee that every distributed copy can always be recalled."],"Ongoing accountability needs a realistic response and honest limits."),
]),
])
